# Copy to .env before `docker compose up`. .env is gitignored; this file is not.
#
# There are no credentials in this file. Spec 003 had three, for the Postgres fold store;
# spec 007 replaced that store with an embedded one on each consumer's own disk plus a
# compacted changelog topic, so there is no longer a server to authenticate to (X5,
# R7.14). Everything below is a lever, and every one has a working default.

# --- spec 007: the local state store and its changelog --------------------------
# Where each consumer keeps its folded state: one directory per OWNED partition, under
# <STATE_DIR>/<group_id>/<partition>/. Compose gives each container its own volume here.
#STATE_DIR=/var/lib/order-state

# Prefix of the per-group changelog topics, `<prefix>.<group_id>`. Changing it means
# re-running scripts/create_topics.sh, which builds the same names from the same default.
#STATE_CHANGELOG_PREFIX=order-fold

# Flip the whole group back to 002's in-process, deliberately-forgetful state:
#   STATE_BACKEND=memory docker compose --profile scale-out up -d
#STATE_BACKEND=local

# How much of the changelog a store replays when it is assigned a partition:
#   full        replay the whole partition every time — the rebuild cost stays visible
#   checkpoint  resume from the offset last restored, so a warm restart reads almost nothing
# Set it on ONE instance to compare the two RESTORED lines.
#STATE_REBUILD=full

# The changelog topics' cleaner knobs, read by scripts/create_topics.sh. Separate from the
# SNAPSHOT_* ones because the two tombstone windows protect different things: the
# snapshot's is how long a lagging consumer can still LEARN of a delete, the changelog's
# is how long a rebuilding store is still told not to RESTORE one.
#FOLD_SEGMENT_MS=10000
#FOLD_MIN_CLEANABLE_DIRTY_RATIO=0.01
#FOLD_DELETE_RETENTION_MS=60000
#FOLD_MIN_COMPACTION_LAG_MS=0

# --- optional levers ------------------------------------------------------------
# Set to any non-empty value to give each notification instance a stable
# group.instance.id, so a restart returns it to its own partitions (002 D10).
#STATIC_MEMBERSHIP=1

# --- spec 004: replication and the producer's durability contract ---------------
# Neither is a credential, so both use the plain `${VAR:-default}` shape rather than
# the `${VAR:?…}` form spec 003 uses for the Postgres settings.

# Replication factor used by scripts/create_topics.sh. Defaults to 3, the broker count.
# Set it to 1 to build the contrast R4.6 exists for — an under-replicated topic on a
# healthy cluster, which loses a partition the moment its one node stops:
#   REPLICATION_FACTOR=1 ORDER_LIFECYCLE_TOPIC=rf1-scratch ./scripts/create_topics.sh
#REPLICATION_FACTOR=3

# How many replicas must acknowledge a write: 0, 1, or all. Defaults to `all`, which is
# what the producer hardcoded from 001 until 004 made it a lever. Anything else fails at
# startup rather than falling back silently.
#   0    returns before the broker has confirmed anything — a lost write is never reported
#   1    the partition leader only — a leader crash before replication still loses it
#   all  every replica currently IN SYNC. Note: with no min.insync.replicas set, an ISR
#        that has shrunk to one member still satisfies this (see docs/replication.md)
#PRODUCER_ACKS=all

# --- spec 005: retries, the dead-letter topic, and the failure lever -------------
# None of these is a credential, so all use the plain `${VAR:-default}` shape.

# The two topics scripts/create_topics.sh creates alongside order-lifecycle. The retry
# topic is read by the retry-worker and nothing else; the dead-letter topic is read by
# NOTHING — that absence is what makes it terminal (005 D10).
#RETRY_TOPIC=order-lifecycle.retry
#DLQ_TOPIC=order-lifecycle.dlq

# Total attempts INCLUDING the first, which the main consumer spends inline. 3 means one
# inline attempt and two in the retry worker, then the dead-letter topic.
#RETRY_MAX_ATTEMPTS=3

# How long each attempt AFTER the first waits, in seconds. Attempt 2 waits the first
# entry, attempt 3 the second; a shorter list reuses its last entry.
#RETRY_BACKOFF_SECONDS=30,120

# The floor under acks=all, and the half of the durability contract 004 left open. With
# it unset, an in-sync set that has shrunk to one member still satisfies acks=all — so an
# acknowledged write can exist in exactly one copy. Read by scripts/create_topics.sh,
# which also ALTERs it onto topics that already exist (005 D12).
#   Stop two of three brokers and place an order to watch a write be refused:
#   docker compose stop kafka-2 kafka-3 && ./scripts/place_orders.sh
#MIN_INSYNC_REPLICAS=2

# The producer's own retry path, which matters once a write can be REFUSED rather than
# merely delayed. message.timeout.ms is the binding one — it caps the total time across
# every retry, and is librdkafka's name for the Java client's delivery.timeout.ms.
#PRODUCER_RETRIES=3
#PRODUCER_RETRY_BACKOFF_MS=100
#PRODUCER_MESSAGE_TIMEOUT_MS=30000

# --- the failure lever (005 D11) ------------------------------------------------
# Set per-experiment, the way 002 sets HANDLER_DELAY_SECONDS and 003 sets
# STATE_CRASH_AFTER. Compose passes all three through from the host and defaults them to
# `none`, so a plain `up` behaves exactly as 004 recorded.
#
#   transient  fails the first HANDLER_FAILURE_ATTEMPTS attempts, then succeeds — so you
#              watch a message recover in the retry worker rather than being told it does
#   poison     fails every time, so the message reaches the dead-letter topic having made
#              exactly one attempt and never touching the retry topic
#
# Leaving HANDLER_FAILURE_ORDERS unset aims the lever at EVERY order, which makes the
# good path unobservable at the same time. Name the orders.
#
# Recreate the consumer AND the retry worker together. The consumer spends attempt 1 and
# the worker spends attempts 2 and 3, so a lever set on only one of them either never
# fails or never keeps failing:
#   HANDLER_FAILURE_MODE=transient HANDLER_FAILURE_ORDERS=<order-id> \
#     docker compose up -d --force-recreate inventory-consumer retry-worker
#HANDLER_FAILURE_MODE=none
#HANDLER_FAILURE_ORDERS=
#HANDLER_FAILURE_ATTEMPTS=2

# --- spec 008: idempotence, transactions, and exactly-once -----------------------
# Two mechanisms, and they are not the same thing. Idempotence stops one producer
# duplicating or reordering its own retries. A transaction makes a consumer's write and
# its offset commit land together or not at all. The first is free and always on; the
# second costs latency and is off by default.

# ON by default, and applies to EVERY producer: the order service's, the failure
# router's, and the changelog's. Duplication this project already absorbs — the sequence
# guard exists for it. REORDERING is what this actually buys: `retries` with the default
# max.in.flight of 5 lets a retried batch land after batches that were behind it, and in
# an ordered lifecycle a SHIPPED that overtakes a PACKED makes every consumer report a
# violation that never happened. On the compacted changelog it is worse — an older fold
# overwrites a newer one, compaction keeps the wrong value, and the rebuild is faithful
# to the corruption. Set false to run as 001–007 did.
#PRODUCER_IDEMPOTENCE=true

# OFF by default, so a plain `up -d` reproduces 007 exactly and stays the control.
# Under exactly_once the changelog write and the offset go into one transaction, which is
# only possible because 007 made both of them Kafka operations.
#   PROCESSING_GUARANTEE=exactly_once docker compose up -d
#
# What it does NOT do is make a handler run once. A redelivery after an abort runs it
# again — notification's customer message prints twice. What it guarantees is that
# nothing which ran twice was ever COMMITTED twice.
#PROCESSING_GUARANTEE=at_least_once

# How much one transaction covers. 1 means one per message, which is what the
# transaction_open crash point below wants; 100 is what a throughput comparison runs
# against. The ms bound is checked on empty polls too, so a transaction opened by the
# last message of a burst cannot hold read_committed readers at the last stable offset
# through a lull.
#TRANSACTION_COMMIT_INTERVAL_MESSAGES=100
#TRANSACTION_COMMIT_INTERVAL_MS=200
#TRANSACTION_TIMEOUT_MS=60000

# read_committed on every consumer, including the changelog restore reader — where it is
# a CORRECTNESS setting, not a preference: a rebuild at read_uncommitted replays aborted
# folds and reintroduces exactly what the transaction was bought to prevent.
#
# Set it to read_uncommitted on a CONSOLE consumer instead. That is the clearest thing in
# this feature: the aborted records are physically on the topic, and the abort is a
# marker over them rather than their absence.
#CONSUMER_ISOLATION_LEVEL=read_committed

# --- CONSUMER_INSTANCE_ID is now correctness-critical (008 D3) -------------------
# It has been a log field since 002. Under exactly_once it becomes half the
# transactional.id (`<group_id>-<instance>`), which is what lets a restarted member fence
# the zombie it replaced. Two members sharing one value fence EACH OTHER in a loop, each
# bumping the epoch the other just took — the consumer logs PRODUCER_FENCED and exits 1.
# docker-compose.yml sets a distinct value on every consumer for this reason.

# --- the transaction crash point (008 D8) ---------------------------------------
# STATE_CRASH_AFTER gains a third value: `transaction_open` fires INSIDE an open
# transaction, after the fold is written and the offset noted and before the commit — the
# window the other two cannot reach, because under a transaction the two writes they sit
# between are no longer separable.
#
#   PROCESSING_GUARANTEE=exactly_once TRANSACTION_COMMIT_INTERVAL_MESSAGES=1 \
#     STATE_CRASH_AFTER=transaction_open \
#     docker compose up -d --force-recreate inventory-consumer
#
# After the crash: group lag is non-zero AND the changelog holds no record for that event
# under read_committed — but the record IS there under read_uncommitted. On restart the
# redelivery leaves handled_count equal to last_sequence, which is the residue 003
# predicted, at zero.
#
# NOTE: STATE_REBUILD=checkpoint is REFUSED under exactly_once and the process exits 2. A
# checkpoint claims the store matches the changelog, and an aborted batch makes that false.
#STATE_CRASH_AFTER=none
